        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked and Tested
          </h2>

          <p>
            Interior paint is sold on colour and on claims, and the claims are hard to compare because every brand uses its own language: one-coat, paint-and-primer, stain-release, scuff-resistant. We ignored the marketing and started with what can be checked: resin type, published coverage and recoat times, VOC information, sheen range, and whether the manufacturer publishes technical data at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because the buy buttons on this site go to Amazon, we gave priority to paints that can genuinely be ordered online and delivered, which rules out most house-brand paints sold only at one big-box chain. We then added two paint-store benchmarks, Benjamin Moore Regal Select and Sherwin-Williams Emerald, clearly marked, so you can see what the extra money buys.
          </p>

          <p>
            Each paint went onto sections of the same primed drywall wall. We rolled a mid-tone colour over a pale base with a 3/8-inch nap roller, noted how many coats it took to hide, and looked for flashing over patched areas. After two weeks of cure we marked the panels with pencil, crayon, coffee and a greasy fingerprint, cleaned them with a damp sponge and mild detergent, and checked for burnishing, which is the shiny patch left when a flat or matte paint is scrubbed.
          </p>

          <p>
            This was a practical, side-by-side test in one house rather than a laboratory scrub machine, and we weigh it alongside manufacturer data and long-term owner reviews. Ratings and review counts are rounded from what we saw across retailers, including the paint-store brands' own sites for the two benchmarks.
          </p>
