        <div id="what-matters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Makes a Wall Paint Good
          </h2>

          <p>
            Paint is pigment, binder, water and additives. Once the water evaporates, only the pigment and binder stay on the wall, and the proportion that stays, called volume solids, is the best single predictor of how a paint will perform. Higher solids means a thicker dry film per coat, which means better hide, fewer coats and more material standing between the wall and whatever you scrub off it later.
          </p>

          <p>
            Binder type is the second thing to check. 100% acrylic binders adhere better, stay more flexible, resist scrubbing and hold colour longer than vinyl-acrylic blends, which are common in the cheapest contractor and builder-grade paints. Every pick on this list is acrylic, and the technical data sheet will tell you if a paint you are considering is not.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hide versus coverage</h3>

          <p>
            These two words are often used interchangeably and they mean different things. Coverage, or spread rate, is how many square feet a gallon will paint, usually 350 to 400 on smooth drywall. Hide is how well a coat obscures the colour underneath. A thin, cheap paint can spread a long way and still need three coats to hide a colour change, which makes it more expensive in practice than a thicker paint that hides in two.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third thing is washability, and it is where sheen and binder quality meet. Scrub resistance is usually tested by running a brush back and forth over the film until it wears through, and better paints last many times more cycles. In a house, the practical questions are simpler: does a scuff come off without leaving a shiny patch, and does the colour survive a sponge. That is the test we care about, and it is where the premium picks pull clearly ahead.
          </p>
        </div>

